WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:05.451
A chilly March wind blew as Patrick suited up for the game at the Scorpions' field.

2
00:00:05.452 --> 00:00:10.268
"Hey, Todd!" He waved at the left fielder, who was warming up with the basemen.

3
00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:16.186
Todd grinned at him. "Hi, Patrick. I bet you're glad to see me back on the field!"

4
00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:21.286
Patrick nodded. "Left field is okay, but I'd much rather catch!"

5
00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:27.440
"Mickey did a good job filling in. He's a great addition to the team," Todd said.

6
00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:34.678
Patrick looked down, adjusting his pads. "Yeah," he said. "See you later."

7
00:00:34.679 --> 00:00:38.109
He sprinted toward home plate to warm up with Dell.

8
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:43.355
"Batter up!" called the umpire a few moments later.

9
00:00:43.356 --> 00:00:50.652
Patrick tried to settle into the familiar place behind the plate, but he felt like all eyes were on him.

10
00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:55.311
Todd wasn't the only one who'd said that Mickey had done a good job as catcher last week. Plus,

11
00:00:55.312 --> 00:00:59.752
Mickey was a fantastic hitter—much better than Patrick. "I

12
00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:07.101
can't mess up today," Patrick thought. He'd spent hours last night preparing for the game by reviewing players' stats. He

13
00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:10.840
probably should have spent just as much time on math. By

14
00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:14.396
the bottom of the seventh inning, the score was tied. The

15
00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:18.467
first Scorpions batter stepped up and swung. Crack!

16
00:01:18.468 --> 00:01:22.232
A single. The

17
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:28.547
next batter stepped into the box. Patrick flicked his gaze toward first base every few seconds. Dell

18
00:01:28.548 --> 00:01:33.560
couldn't easily see the runner, so it was up to Patrick to watch him closely. If

19
00:01:33.561 --> 00:01:38.550
the runner tried to steal a base, Patrick had to signal Dell quickly. Before

20
00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:47.535
Dell released the pitch, the runner started toward second, taking a long lead. Could Dell turn around and pick him off? "Not

21
00:01:47.536 --> 00:01:54.552
worth the risk," decided Patrick. He signaled for a curve ball, and Dell whipped it in. "Strike!"

22
00:01:54.553 --> 00:01:56.061
The

23
00:01:56.062 --> 00:01:59.859
runner dashed back to first base and took his lead again. Patrick

24
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.103
could see Dell get ready for the pick, waiting for Patrick's signal. The

25
00:02:04.104 --> 00:02:10.767
runner inched closer toward second. He was really pushing it. Patrick

26
00:02:10.768 --> 00:02:15.186
considered trying to pick him off, but the runner was fast. Patrick

27
00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:19.273
knew that a mistake on the field would put the runner on second. So

28
00:02:19.274 --> 00:02:23.924
Patrick signaled the pitch, and Dell delivered the fastball. Whack!

29
00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:32.433
The ball rocketed far out to center field. The runner on first base rounded second and slid into third. By

30
00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:38.748
the end of the inning, the runner had scored. The Scorpions won the game, 5-4. Patrick was

31
00:02:38.749 --> 00:02:45.089
standing in the dugout with some other players when Daniel stalked over. "What's the

32
00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.999
matter with you, Patrick?" Daniel demanded. "That guy

33
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.479
was an easy pick. You should have signaled Dell." Patrick kept

34
00:02:53.480 --> 00:03:00.248
his eyes down. Daniel was right. Patrick and Dell should have picked off the runner. "Everybody makes

35
00:03:00.249 --> 00:03:04.976
mistakes," Patrick said in a low voice. Mickey clapped

36
00:03:04.977 --> 00:03:10.727
Patrick on the shoulder. "Shake it off, dude. We'll win the next one." Patrick shrugged

37
00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:13.715
Mickey's hand away. "Players don't

38
00:03:13.716 --> 00:03:19.482
lose," Mickey went on. "Teams lose. It's not your fault." "Yes, it

39
00:03:19.483 --> 00:03:23.805
is my fault." Patrick glared at Mickey. "I was

40
00:03:23.806 --> 00:03:29.302
rusty today. That's from being stuck in left field last week." Dell looked

41
00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:33.703
surprised. "Hey, Patrick. Ease up a little." "Yeah," Daniel

42
00:03:33.704 --> 00:03:37.390
chimed in. "Knock it off." "It's been

43
00:03:37.391 --> 00:03:42.967
a bad day." Patrick sighed. "How about just giving me some space?" "Sure." Dell

44
00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:49.363
shook his head and walked away. Mickey and Daniel went to talk with some other players. Patrick felt

45
00:03:49.364 --> 00:03:55.767
Daniel's old mitt in his bag as he stuffed his other equipment inside. "This thing

46
00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:59.764
is more like an unlucky mitt," he thought. He should

47
00:03:59.765 --> 00:04:03.343
give it to Buff for a chew toy. * *

48
00:04:03.344 --> 00:04:10.178
* Patrick avoided his friends at school the next morning. He felt embarrassed by the way he had acted after the game

49
00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:16.343
yesterday. "But it wasn't all your fault," he thought. Daniel shouldn't have criticized Patrick in front of

50
00:04:16.344 --> 00:04:26.928
everyone. He slid into a seat at an empty lunch table. He shoveled down the cafeteria's "Friday Special" and pulled out his math book.

51
00:04:26.929 --> 00:04:33.562
He had a test in 25—no, 24—minutes, and he definitely wasn't ready for it.

52
00:04:33.563 --> 00:04:39.074
He stared at his textbook. Did anyone really need to know about exponents?

53
00:04:39.075 --> 00:04:42.825
"I need to know or I'll flunk this test," he muttered.

54
00:04:42.826 --> 00:04:50.300
"You're kidding!" Dell's voice suddenly rose from a nearby table. He laughed loudly.

55
00:04:50.301 --> 00:04:56.465
Patrick looked to his left. Most of the members of the baseball team were gathered at the other table, talking to Coach.

56
00:04:56.466 --> 00:05:02.210
Mickey said something, and then Dell laughed loudly again, and motioned toward where Patrick sat.

57
00:05:02.211 --> 00:05:09.629
Patrick's face burned. Were they laughing at him? Maybe they were making fun of him because of his mom's van.

58
00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:14.208
Or, more likely, they were saying that his catching stunk yesterday.

59
00:05:14.209 --> 00:05:19.950
Patrick kept his eyes on his book and pretended he didn't hear them. But it wasn't easy.

60
00:05:19.951 --> 00:05:27.133
They kept talking and laughing loudly. Finally Patrick closed the book in frustration and put his head on top of it.

61
00:05:27.134 --> 00:05:29.845
"Test today?" asked a deep voice.

62
00:05:29.846 --> 00:05:34.441
Patrick raised his head. Coach loomed over him like a skyscraper.

63
00:05:34.442 --> 00:05:38.287
"I was just studying," Patrick said.

64
00:05:38.288 --> 00:05:45.177
Coach glanced at the closed book and raised his eyebrows. "I find it easier to study with my book open

65
00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:46.998
." "I'm just

66
00:05:46.999 --> 00:05:52.429
tired." Coach sat across the table from Patrick. "I heard you were having some problems in

67
00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:57.253
math." "What?" Patrick thought. Coach knew about his math

68
00:05:57.254 --> 00:06:00.253
grade? He glanced at the table where his teammates

69
00:06:00.254 --> 00:06:04.657
sat. Dell was the only one that Patrick had told about his last math

70
00:06:04.658 --> 00:06:08.502
quiz. "I can't believe Dell ratted on me!" Patrick thought.

71
00:06:08.503 --> 00:06:11.769
"The school has a tutoring program," continued Coach.

72
00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:18.148
"And strict rules about athletes' grades. So get some help sooner rather than later.

73
00:06:18.149 --> 00:06:23.488
Another failing quiz grade, Patrick, and you won't be able to play baseball."

74
00:06:23.489 --> 00:06:30.669
Patrick swallowed hard. "I'll do better, Coach. I promise." His face burned again.

75
00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:35.308
The whole team was watching as Coach lectured him about his crummy grades.

76
00:06:35.309 --> 00:06:41.037
"Good!" said Coach as the bell rang. "See you later at practice."

77
00:06:41.038 --> 00:06:43.405
"Yes, sir," said Patrick.

78
00:06:43.406 --> 00:06:49.625
Patrick ignored his teammates at the other table. He was still steaming over how they'd been talking about him.

79
00:06:49.626 --> 00:06:57.126
He broke out in a sweat, and suddenly he could feel the cafeteria's "Friday Special" rising in his stomach.

80
00:06:57.127 --> 00:07:04.673
He clamped a hand over his mouth and ran for the exit.

